		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>uPVC windows, doors and conservatory frames stay looking new for years if you clean them with the right products and a light touch. In Hull and East Yorkshire, the combination of rain, road grime and hard water leaves chalky streaks on glass and green algae on north-facing frames, especially after winter.</p>
<p>This guide covers a safe routine for frames, sills, handles and conservatory roof panels, what to avoid so you don’t scratch plastic or damage seals, and when it’s worth booking help for a whole-house refresh.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Use warm water with a small amount of washing-up liquid or a dedicated uPVC cleaner — not abrasive pads or undiluted bleach on frames.</li><li>Dry glass with a squeegee and microfibre to avoid hard-water spotting.</li><li>Green algae on conservatory roofs needs a stable ladder, soft brush and patience; don’t pressure-wash seals.</li><li>Frames and sills are often included in a <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular house clean</a> if you ask in advance.</li></ul></div>
<h2>What you need</h2>
<ul>
<li>Two buckets (wash and rinse), soft sponge or microfibre cloth</li>
<li>Non-scratch brush for corners and drainage channels</li>
<li>Squeegee and dry microfibre for glass</li>
<li>Step ladder with someone footing it if you’re reaching high frames</li>
</ul>
<h2>Cleaning uPVC frames and sills</h2>
<p>Brush loose dirt from sills and drainage slots first — blocked channels cause dripping and staining. Wash frames top to bottom with warm soapy water, then rinse. Wipe dry so water doesn’t run onto walls and leave tide marks.</p>
<p>Avoid steel wool, kitchen scourers and strong solvent cleaners: they dull the finish and can attack rubber gaskets. If mould appears on seals, use a mould remover suitable for bathrooms and rinse well. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:damp-and-mould-in-older-homes}}">condensation and mould in older homes</a> explains why it keeps coming back on cold reveals.</p>
<h2>Glass without streaks</h2>
<p>Clean on a cloudy day if you can — sun dries product too fast. Wash, squeegee, then buff edges with a dry cloth. Chalky spotting is often <a href="{{guide:hard-water-limescale-east-yorkshire}}">hard water</a>; a dilute vinegar rinse on glass only can help, but keep it off bare metal handles.</p>
<h2>Conservatory roofs</h2>
<p>Polycarbonate or glass roof panels collect pollen, bird droppings and algae. Work from the house outward, section by section, and never lean on glazing bars. If the roof is steep or high, professional equipment is safer — many customers pair exterior frame work with a <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> inside after winter.</p>
<h2>How often to clean uPVC</h2>
<p>Frames and sills: every two to three months, or monthly if you’re on a busy road or near the coast. Glass: as needed; most households manage every four to six weeks. For a full reset, see <a href="{{guide:deep-clean-vs-regular-clean}}">deep clean vs regular clean</a>.</p>
<h2>Help in Hull and East Yorkshire</h2>
<p>Our team covers <a href="{{area:hull}}">Hull</a>, <a href="{{area:beverley}}">Beverley</a>, <a href="{{area:cottingham}}">Cottingham</a> and surrounding villages. We can include frames, sills and interior glass as part of <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular cleaning</a> or a one-off visit — <a href="{{page:contact}}">contact us</a> for a fixed quote.</p>
